@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Code 5: notebooks)

`@@notebook` is a Jupyter-style notebook: cells share one kernel, so a name set in one cell is there in the next.

@section(Cells sharing a kernel)

`kernel=` is python or js; a bare @@cell keeps the previous cell's language.

@notebook(kernel=python)
@cell
x = 21
@cell
print(x * 2)
@end

@section(Text, locked setup and IDoc cells)

`@@cell(markdown)` is formatted text, `@@cell(python, locked)` is setup a student cannot edit, and `@@cell(idoc)` is live IDoc, even a question.

@notebook(kernel=python)
@cell(markdown)
# Warm-up
Run the cells in order.
@cell(python, locked)
import math
@cell
print(round(math.pi, 3))
@cell(idoc)
@q(fn: 3.142) What did the cell print?
@end

@section(A JavaScript notebook)

@notebook(kernel=js)
@cell
let n = 5;
@cell
console.log(n * n);
@end

@section(Size, timing and marking)

`height=`/`h=`, `width=`/`w=`, `minwidth=`/`maxwidth=` (`minw=`/`maxw=`) and `x=`/`y=` size and place it; `benchmark=` times a run; `id=` and `time=` make it a live-class exercise. Marks go on each graded cell, as `pts=` on the cell.

@notebook(kernel=python, height=320, width=90%, maxwidth=900, minwidth=300, benchmark=nb_ms, id=nb1, time=10m)
@cell
data = [3, 5, 7]
@cell(python, pts=2, expect="5.0")
print(sum(data) / len(data))
@end

@notebook(kernel=python, h=200, w=80%, minw=250, maxw=700, x=0, y=0)
@cell
print("short size names")
@end
